{
  "id": "xoe_khap_thai_northwest_vietnam",
  "name": "Xòe and khắp (Thái dance-song of northwest Vietnam)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Song and dance of the Thái (Tai Dam and Tai Don) people of the northwestern uplands of Vietnam, in Sơn La, Điện Biên, Lai Châu and the Mường Lò valley of Yên Bái. Xòe, inscribed on UNESCO's Representative List as the Art of Xòe dance of the Tai people, is danced in ritual, circle and presentational forms at rituals, weddings and village festivals, the circle form with joined hands around a fire or a jar of rice wine; khắp is Thái sung poetry performed at festivals, weddings and courtship. The music is played on the tính tẩu gourd lute, the khèn bè mouth organ, reed pipes, drums, gongs and cymbals in a steady duple beat; the khaen card stands in for the khèn bè and generic drum, gong and cymbal cards for the percussion, and the gourd lute has no card.",
  "recipe": "classic modern xòe and khắp, groove Percussion, east Asian branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. khaen, membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum, gong tamtam, credited cymbals. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
